A||||8||Getaway2k||Trans cooler line question||||1||
Z||000000||Getaway2k||11-19-2000||04:23 PM||||Get ready for a good laugh. I am in the process of replacing the 2 rubber transmission cooler line hoses (run from the trans to the metal trans lines). Well, I have both of the rubber lines off, problem is, I can't remember if the 8 inch hose connected to the top line that comes out of the transmission or the bottom line. The other hose is 26 inches long and connects to the metal trans line that runs along the inner fender (again, I'm not sure if the other end connects to the top or bottom line that sticks out of the transmission. It's an 86 GT with an automatic transmission. Anyone willing to crawl under their car to look for me?!<P>Thanks-||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||11-19-2000||10:29 PM||||So, you really hosed it up this time, huh? Hehehehe....ahem!  Good laugh, until it's <B>your</B> problem, eh?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  <P>Getaway, I just looked at my 1986 SE V6 Automatic.  Here's what I found:<P>The <B>short</B> hose runs from the <B>bottom</B> transaxle fitting, over to a metal line on the sidewall of the engine compartment. <P>The <B>long</B> hose runs from the <B>top</B> trans. fitting, turns aft and clips to a long bracket attached to the top of the trans. (the bracket sticks way the heck out there, towards driver's side of car). From there, the hose loops down and then forward, into a sheath, and runs forward underneath the car.       <P>BTW, didn't even have to crawl underneath the car.  It was all visible by looking under the hood  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000002||Eric_PA||11-19-2000||11:23 PM||||I just replace mine today on my 86. They are the way Stugood described. I lost all my fluid this week in front of my place and found that the line was cut at the clamp so decided to replace both of them. Now my altenator bracket cracked again. I allready had that thing tig welded once. I thought it was weird that someone posted this since I just went through it.<P>Eric<BR>86.5 Black fastback<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Getaway2k||11-20-2000||07:18 PM||||StuGood...where does the sheath go? Is it over the part of the hose that goes into the bracket or does the sheath go on the hose after the bracket? I can't tell what/where the sheath is supposed to protect the hose from. <P>Thanks for all of your help!||||reg||1
Z||000004||Getaway2k||11-20-2000||09:02 PM||||Hey Eric..talk about "weird", we're both from Pa and we both have a black 86 GT Fastback.<P>Strange days indeed..!<p>[This message has been edited by Getaway2k (edited 11-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||11-20-2000||11:31 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by StuGood:<BR><B>...the hose loops down and then forward, into a sheath, and runs forward underneath the car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sheath goes on after the bracket.  From what I could tell, the sheath just seems to protect the hose from chafing on the bottom of the car.<P>BTW, do you suppose there is a serial trans. hose saboteur, lurking around in Pennsylvania  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?   <P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Getaway2k||11-21-2000||10:11 AM||||I think that the problem has something to do with GREMLINS!!<P>Either that or the Government is behind it!<P>Thanks for all of your help. I can now finish the job today.||||reg||1
